1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,240
Harare, 2016. It's five in the morning,

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:08,400
and the queue outside the bank
already stretches around the corner.

3
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:11,200
Let's call the woman near the front Rudo.

4
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:14,720
She's a teacher. And when she
finally reaches the counter,

5
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:18,080
the most she can take
out is fifty dollars.

6
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:20,400
So Rudo stops queuing.

7
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:24,960
She takes out her phone,
dials star, one five one, hash,

8
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:28,160
and pays for her bread
with a few button presses.

9
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:31,200
She wasn't alone. By 2017,

10
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:35,840
more than ninety-six percent of the
value of all transactions in Zimbabwe was

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,520
electronic. Cash had almost disappeared,

12
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:44,640
and one phone service, EcoCash,
carried most of the country's payments.

13
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:49,360
Then, in June 2020,
the government suspended it.

14
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:55,040
So how did a phone code take over a
country's money, and what went wrong?

15
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:59,680
Let's find out. EcoCash
launched in September 2011,

16
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:02,560
run by Zimbabwe's Econet Wireless.

17
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:06,600
The idea was simple. Your
phone number becomes a wallet.

18
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:10,773
You hand cash to an agent,
a shop or a kiosk on the corner,

19
00:01:10,827 --> 00:01:13,280
and the money appears on your phone.

20
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:17,960
Then you can send it, pay with it,
or take it out again somewhere else.

21
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,200
And Zimbabwe loved it.

22
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:23,920
People who signed up at the
start even got a free dollar.

23
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:28,920
Within two months, one hundred and
sixty-five thousand people had registered.

24
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:31,880
By March 2012, one million.

25
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:33,640
By late 2013,

26
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:38,840
about three million users had moved
two point two billion US dollars.

27
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:43,160
In 2014, the code got even shorter: star,

28
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:47,160
one five one, hash. By the middle of 2016,

29
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:52,360
EcoCash had six million customers
and twenty-six thousand agents.

30
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:55,787
But the real turning point
wasn't a marketing campaign.

31
00:01:55,813 --> 00:02:01,640
It was a crisis. Since 2009,
Zimbabwe had used the US dollar.

32
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:04,453
But a country can't print US dollars.

33
00:02:04,507 --> 00:02:08,320
And by 2016, there simply
weren't enough of them.

34
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:10,360
Banks ran short of notes.

35
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:14,880
The queues grew. Withdrawals were
capped at fifty dollars a day,

36
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:17,320
and one hundred and fifty a week.

37
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:21,560
In November 2016, the central
bank released bond notes,

38
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:26,440
two and five dollar notes that were
supposed to be worth the same as US dollars.

39
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:29,400
But people had already found another way.

40
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:32,200
They moved their money onto their phones.

41
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:38,000
In 2016, mobile money was already eighty-one
percent of all electronic payments by

42
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:42,680
number. By 2017, according to
the Reserve Bank of Zimbabwe,

43
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:46,600
over ninety-six percent of
transaction value was electronic,

44
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:49,120
and of about one billion transactions,

45
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:53,440
seven hundred and fifty-four
million were made on a mobile phone.

46
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:55,840
Shops, markets, bills.

47
00:02:55,920 --> 00:03:00,400
More and more of daily life
ran on star one five one hash.

48
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:04,640
Zimbabwe had gone almost
cashless, almost by accident.

49
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:06,520
But there was a catch.

50
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:11,200
A dollar on your phone was not
the same as a dollar in your hand.

51
00:03:12,068 --> 00:03:15,748
Because cash was so scarce,
people started selling it.

52
00:03:16,308 --> 00:03:19,508
If you wanted one hundred
US dollars in notes,

53
00:03:19,588 --> 00:03:23,988
you paid more than one hundred
dollars by EcoCash or bank transfer.

54
00:03:24,388 --> 00:03:26,148
By early 2018,

55
00:03:26,228 --> 00:03:30,468
newspapers reported that premium
had reached sixty-five percent.

56
00:03:31,108 --> 00:03:35,668
So the same loaf of bread now
had two prices: one in cash,

57
00:03:35,708 --> 00:03:37,508
and a higher one on your phone.

58
00:03:38,388 --> 00:03:40,868
Then, in October 2018,

59
00:03:40,948 --> 00:03:44,948
a new two percent tax arrived
on electronic transfers.

60
00:03:45,268 --> 00:03:48,988
Many EcoCash payments
now cost a little extra.

61
00:03:49,508 --> 00:03:54,308
In February 2019, the electronic
dollars got their own name:

62
00:03:54,428 --> 00:03:56,148
the RTGS dollar.

63
00:03:56,948 --> 00:04:01,588
By June, the Zimbabwe dollar
was the only legal tender again.

64
00:04:01,708 --> 00:04:04,708
And prices began to climb fast.

65
00:04:04,988 --> 00:04:07,348
Agents were caught in the middle.

66
00:04:07,428 --> 00:04:09,348
In September 2019,

67
00:04:09,428 --> 00:04:14,468
EcoCash itself suspended more than four
thousand agents for charging people a

68
00:04:14,748 --> 00:04:19,428
premium to cash out. Many of the people
living through this had family in South

69
00:04:19,508 --> 00:04:21,988
Africa, sending support home.

70
00:04:22,468 --> 00:04:25,988
And that brings us to the
moment everything changed.

71
00:04:26,308 --> 00:04:28,388
But first, a quick word from us.

72
00:04:29,028 --> 00:04:33,428
If you're in South Africa, you can send
airtime and data home with Fluxr.

73
00:04:33,988 --> 00:04:37,508
Buy a 1Voucher, OTT Voucher, Blu Voucher,

74
00:04:37,828 --> 00:04:41,828
FNB Voucher or 4ALL
Voucher at your local store,

75
00:04:41,908 --> 00:04:47,748
then dial star one three zero,
star three one zero two six, star,

76
00:04:47,828 --> 00:04:49,668
your voucher PIN, hash.

77
00:04:49,988 --> 00:04:52,548
Top up your own phone
here in South Africa,

78
00:04:52,668 --> 00:04:56,068
or send airtime and data
to family back home.

79
00:04:56,148 --> 00:04:59,508
It works on any phone, no data needed.

80
00:04:59,588 --> 00:05:04,708
Or get the Fluxr app on Google Play:
turn your voucher into wallet credit,

81
00:05:04,788 --> 00:05:09,348
then buy airtime, data and
electricity, here and back home,

82
00:05:09,468 --> 00:05:13,348
plus vouchers and bill
payments, all in one app.

83
00:05:13,428 --> 00:05:16,628
Now, back to June 2020.

84
00:05:16,708 --> 00:05:19,268
On the twenty-sixth of June 2020,

85
00:05:19,308 --> 00:05:23,828
the government halted mobile money
transactions and suspended the Zimbabwe Stock

86
00:05:24,108 --> 00:05:26,868
Exchange, all on the same day.

87
00:05:26,948 --> 00:05:31,828
It said EcoCash handled about ninety-four
percent of those transactions.

88
00:05:31,868 --> 00:05:35,348
And it accused mobile money
of feeding the black market,

89
00:05:35,468 --> 00:05:40,388
where cash was being sold at
premiums of thirty to fifty percent.

90
00:05:40,468 --> 00:05:43,108
The next day, the order was narrowed.

91
00:05:43,228 --> 00:05:45,468
Ordinary people could still pay.

92
00:05:45,508 --> 00:05:48,748
But agent lines and bulk
payments were suspended,

93
00:05:48,788 --> 00:05:53,508
so the corner kiosks could no
longer turn phone money into cash.

94
00:05:53,588 --> 00:05:58,308
Weeks later, police searched EcoCash's
head office in Harare for transaction

95
00:05:58,788 --> 00:06:01,748
records. Econet challenged it in court.

96
00:06:01,788 --> 00:06:05,628
And in August 2020, new rules arrived.

97
00:06:05,668 --> 00:06:08,841
Each person could have
just one EcoCash line,

98
00:06:08,895 --> 00:06:12,628
with a limit of five thousand
Zimbabwe dollars a day.

99
00:06:12,828 --> 00:06:14,548
Agent wallets were abolished.

100
00:06:14,628 --> 00:06:18,828
For Rudo, that meant the
kiosk on her corner was gone.

101
00:06:19,108 --> 00:06:21,108
And by the end of 2020,

102
00:06:21,188 --> 00:06:26,268
the regulator counted about five and
a half million active EcoCash users.

103
00:06:26,548 --> 00:06:28,388
That was fewer than before,

104
00:06:28,468 --> 00:06:33,348
but EcoCash still handled almost all
of the country's mobile money payments.

105
00:06:34,237 --> 00:06:36,717
So where is EcoCash today?

106
00:06:37,037 --> 00:06:41,117
It's still here, and it's
still star one five one hash.

107
00:06:41,437 --> 00:06:46,917
In 2024, the EcoCash service moved
directly into Econet Wireless.

108
00:06:47,197 --> 00:06:51,837
The listed company that used to carry
its name became TN CyberTech in

109
00:06:51,917 --> 00:06:56,677
2025. But the wallet in
people's pockets kept its name.

110
00:06:57,037 --> 00:06:59,677
Zimbabwe's money changed again too.

111
00:06:59,757 --> 00:07:04,397
In April 2024, it launched
a new currency, ZiG,

112
00:07:04,477 --> 00:07:08,877
Zimbabwe Gold.
And EcoCash is growing again.

113
00:07:08,997 --> 00:07:14,477
In May 2025, it started sending money
out of Zimbabwe to other countries.

114
00:07:14,517 --> 00:07:17,197
In its year to February 2026,

115
00:07:17,237 --> 00:07:22,477
Econet said revenue from mobile money
services grew sixty-three percent.

116
00:07:22,557 --> 00:07:25,357
Money also keeps coming in from outside.

117
00:07:25,437 --> 00:07:30,477
The Reserve Bank says Zimbabweans abroad
sent home about two point four five billion

118
00:07:30,557 --> 00:07:35,397
US dollars in 2025. So what went wrong?

119
00:07:35,757 --> 00:07:38,797
EcoCash solved one problem brilliantly.

120
00:07:38,837 --> 00:07:42,184
It made it easy to pay
when there was no cash.

121
00:07:42,210 --> 00:07:47,570
But it couldn't solve the bigger problem:
people didn't trust the money itself.

122
00:07:47,624 --> 00:07:51,317
When the cash ran out,
the phone kept the country trading.

123
00:07:51,357 --> 00:07:55,277
But when the money on the phone and the
money in your hand stopped being worth the

124
00:07:55,357 --> 00:07:58,530
same, the phone was caught in the middle.

125
00:07:58,584 --> 00:08:01,944
The lesson? Moving money is easy.

126
00:08:02,050 --> 00:08:04,597
Keeping its value is the hard part.

127
00:08:04,637 --> 00:08:08,597
A wallet is only as strong
as the money inside it.

128
00:08:08,637 --> 00:08:14,557
Let's recap. EcoCash launched in 2011
and reached a million users in about six

129
00:08:15,037 --> 00:08:18,117
months. When dollars ran short in 2016,

130
00:08:18,157 --> 00:08:21,144
Zimbabweans moved their
money onto their phones,

131
00:08:21,170 --> 00:08:26,757
and by 2017 over ninety-six percent
of transaction value was electronic.

132
00:08:26,797 --> 00:08:31,410
But cash and phone money drifted
apart, a two percent tax arrived,

133
00:08:31,464 --> 00:08:36,397
and in June 2020 the government
suspended mobile money and later cut its

134
00:08:36,477 --> 00:08:40,917
agents. EcoCash survived,
and it's still growing.

135
00:08:40,957 --> 00:08:44,357
But Zimbabwe's story shows
that a phone can move money,

136
00:08:44,397 --> 00:08:46,997
but it can't make people trust it.

137
00:08:47,037 --> 00:08:50,197
If you enjoyed this story,
subscribe for the next one.

138
00:08:50,237 --> 00:08:53,797
And if you're in South Africa,
sending airtime and data home,

139
00:08:53,837 --> 00:08:55,357
you know where to find us.

